            <ArticleTitle>Managing malignant spinal cord compression.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>859-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Spinal malignancy can present as low back pain and accounts for most cases of spinal cord compression (SCC).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assist the clinician in the diagnosis of malignant SCC and to increase awareness of various treatment modalities. A clinical approach for the workup of patients with 'metastatic carcinoma of unknown primary' is also outlined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is important to diagnose SCC early and initiate urgent treatment to obtain optimum patient outcomes. Further management includes workup for the primary tumour source if unknown.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
